<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Langston Hughes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as James Mercer Langston Hughes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Langston Hughes was an American poet, social activist, novelist, playwright and columnist from Joplin, Missouri, an early innovator of jazz poetry and a leader of the Harlem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. His poem The Negro Speaks of Rivers appeared in The Crisis in 1921, and his first collection, The Weary Blues, in 1926. He also wrote plays, short stories, novels and nonfiction, and from 1943 published stories about Jesse B. Semple, an everyday black man in Harlem. From 1942 to 1962, as the civil rights movement grew, he wrote a weekly column for The Chicago Defender. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1967 after surgery related to prostate cancer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/langston-hughes.jpg" alt="Langston Hughes" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Van Vechten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langston_Hughes_by_Carl_Van_Vechten_Trim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Langston Hughes was born in Joplin, Missouri, on 1 February 1901, though in his autobiography he gave 1902. Like many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> he was of mixed ancestry: both his paternal great-grandmothers were enslaved Africans and both paternal great-grandfathers white slave owners in Kentucky. His maternal grandmother, Mary Patterson, was one of the first women to attend Oberlin College. Her first husband, Lewis Sheridan Leary, was fatally wounded in John Brown's raid on Harpers Ferry in 1859, and her second, Charles Henry Langston, was an abolitionist.</p><p>Hughes had a poor relationship with his father. After high school in 1920 he lived with him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and they agreed that Hughes would study engineering if he could attend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a>. He enrolled in September 1921 but dropped out in 1922, having met racial prejudice and being drawn more to Harlem than to his studies. He eventually graduated from Lincoln University.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hughes's poem The Negro Speaks of Rivers appeared in 1921 in The Crisis, the magazine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">National Association for the Advancement of Colored People</a>, and became his signature poem. It was collected in his first book, The Weary Blues, published in 1926. His work was influential during the Harlem Renaissance, alongside Zora Neale Hurston, Wallace Thurman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claude-mckay">Claude McKay</a> and Countee Cullen, with whom he worked on the short-lived magazine Fire!!</p><p>Hughes and his contemporaries tried to depict the real lives of Black people in the lower social and economic strata. His essay The Negro Artist and the Racial Mountain, published in The Nation in 1926, is regarded as their manifesto. In the 1930s his political writing often engaged with left-wing and Communist-aligned ideas. In 1932 he travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> for a proposed film on Black life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which was abandoned, and in 1937 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> as a correspondent during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. From 1942 to 1962 he wrote a weekly column for The Chicago Defender.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hughes died in New York City on 22 May 1967, aged 66, from complications following abdominal surgery related to prostate cancer. His ashes are interred beneath a floor medallion in the foyer of the Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture in Harlem, a cosmogram titled Rivers. His house at 20 East 127th Street became a New York City landmark in 1981 and was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Langston Hughes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shuffle Along, a musical of 1921 produced, composed, directed and written by African Americans, ran for 504 performances on Broadway; <b>Langston Hughes</b> later wrote that Manhattan’s Black Renaissance “began with Shuffle Along”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Harlem Renaissance of the 1920s and 1930s, whose writers, among them <b>Langston Hughes</b>, Claude McKay, Alain Locke and W.E.B. Du Bois, addressed race relations and double consciousness, was a further influence.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Golden Gate Quartet recorded it in 1957, Louis Armstrong for the album Louis and the Good Book in 1958, and the Robert Shaw Chorale in 1964, with liner notes by <b>Langston Hughes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/go-down-moses">Go Down Moses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Joplin, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.08,-94.51&amp;year=1943" aria-label="Joplin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Langston Hughes"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4692 4390h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4692 4390h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.08,-94.51&amp;year=1943">What was here in 1943? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
